 "meta": {
  "title": "routing question set",
  "version": "20261119_083359",
  "written_by": "one AI labeller (Claude Opus 5.5), in English; questions phrased as a clinician or a developer might write them, not sampled from real traffic",
  "blind_to_implementation": "Written without reading the router implementation (router.py) or the service tests. Sources read: the service README.md and openapi.yaml, and the formal specification (inputs I1-I6, decision table R0-R7, invariants INV-1 to INV-5, failure modes F1-F7).",
  "categories": {
   "a": "verify: check a code the caller already holds (task verify)",
   "b": "retrieve: find the code for a term (task retrieve)",
   "c": "hierarchy: parents, children or subsumption (task hierarchy)",
   "d": "map: a code from one system to another (task map)",
   "e": "interpret: open clinical reasoning, with no code emitted (task interpret)",
   "f": "out of scope: the service should abstain (no task class fits)"
  },
  "expected_route_rule": "The route a correct router reaches under the specification: R0 verify; R1 one exact candidate; R2 expand-then-lookup when there is none, followed by the route of the name the base uses (R2→R1, R2→R3) or by R7 when no concept has the meaning; R3 several candidates; R-pre when no vocabulary is named; R4 hierarchy; R5 map; R6 interpret; R7 abstain.",
  "candidate_rule": "For retrieve questions with a named vocabulary, the class is measured in the local base that holds it, over standard and valid concepts: one = exactly one concept whose name equals the term (case and spacing ignored); several = more than one such concept, or none such and more than one whose name contains every word of the term; none = no concept whose name contains every word. This is the labeller's reading of the public table, not the store's matcher, which was not read. The README example (93832-4 in LOINC, R1) holds under it: one exact standard concept, against two among all concepts.",
  "borderline_rule": "borderline = a second labeller with the same definitions could choose another category or another expected route; the reason names the alternative. A question with two operations takes the category of the first and is borderline.",
  "store_note": "Neither local base holds LOINC and SNOMED together: Athena holds LOINC and UCUM, Vocab2 holds SNOMED, ICD-10 and UCUM (counts in local_bases). Each question names the base that holds its vocabulary; with a store that lacks it, the specification expects an abstention that names the gap (INV-4).",
  "request_fields": "system, codes, display, term and target_system hold only what the question states (term is the free-text phrase a caller would send); task follows the category, and is null for (f).",
  "limits": "written and labelled by one AI labeller; English only; n small; not sampled from real use.",
